  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA weak mid/upper level circulation near the AR/TN border is \ndrifting slowly east. A weak midlevel deformation zone out ahead \nof it is enhancing shower and thunderstorm activity over our area \ntoday. Flow is weak and PWAT values high, and like recent days \nthese slow-moving storms can produce locally heavy\nrainfall/flooding and strong gusty winds. Sunday looks similar as\nthe weak mid/upper low transitions to an open wave and continues \nto drift east. Weak flow, PWAT values in the 1.8 to 2 inch range \nand MLCAPE values generally in the 1000-1500 J/kg range point to \nanother day with a potential for a few storms to become strong to \nmarginally severe, with strong winds the primary threat along with\nlocalized flooding.\n\nModels have not been consistent on how quickly the trough will exit \neast, but the trend has it moving east more quickly leading to the \npattern becoming more northwesterly aloft for Monday and Tuesday \nwith a broad upper trough centered to our east and a broad high \npressure ridge to our west. This brings slightly drier air aloft to \nthe area, steeper mid-level lapse rates, and afternoon MLCAPE values \nmore in the 1500 to 2000 J/kg range. Shear still generally looks \nweak, but given the better thermodynamic profile storms Monday and \nTuesday could pose a slightly higher threat of becoming strong to \nsevere with damaging winds still the primary threat.  \n\nModels diverge for the later periods with cluster analysis showing \none camp of ensemble members keeping us in more of a northwest flow \naloft while another camp builds the ridge east with the jet \ndisplaced further north, thus confidence on the details is low. \nConvective coverage will be influenced by which camp is more correct \nas well as any short waves moving through the flow. Ensembles \nsuggest at least a slight uptick in shear especially north by mid-\nweek which is more consistent with the northwest flow camp of which \nsome (including the latest runs of the operational GFS) bring a \nsignificant wave and a cold front into and through the area late in \nthe work week. Overall, daily chances for showers and storms can be \nexpected, but specifics on timing and severe/flooding potential will \nhave to wait until we get closer.",
